asset prices have been crashing and  there's a lot of investors wondering  what's going to happen in the future  well we can use history as a guide  doesn't necessarily tell us exactly  what's going to happen but it sure does  give us a lot of context chris dixon is  one of those investors who's been around  through multiple market cycles he  recently had a tweet thread that  explained exactly what his thoughts were  around the 2008 global financial crisis  and how it had an impact on various  technologies and companies that were  built here he starts it off and he says  there's a lot of talk lately about the  possibility of a prolonged financial  downturn reminiscent of 2008 2008 was a  difficult time for many many people but  from a startup perspective 2008 and the  following three years turned out to be a  golden age apple released the iphone in  2007 and the app store in 2008 by 2009  talented founders they were pouring in  most of today's top mobile apps were  created by companies founded between  2009 and 2011 including uber venmo snap  and instagram in retrospect that era was  propelled by a combination of three  powerful trends social media cloud  computing and the rise of smartphones  independent product design spaces are  multiplicative even if social cloud and  mobile each improved linearly the  combination could improve exponentially  chris included this chart that he made  to think about product and financial  cycles and what he's done here is he's  overlaid the nasdaq index with the key  projects and companies and their  founding dates and then this idea of  product cycles everything from the  incubation phase all the way through to  the growth phase chris then went on to  talk about the key idea is that product  and financial cycles evolve mostly  independently the top of the chart shows  the nasdaq index as a rough proxy for  financial sentiment financial sentiment  fluctuates unpredictably and sometimes  wildly the next row shows the years that  iconic startups or products were created  the timing is mostly dictated by product  cycles which are shown in that bottom  row product cycles follow their own  internal logic and tend to be more  predictable than financial cycles  they begin with an incubation phase in  which enthusiasts explore ideas and  build products that are mostly used by  other enthusiasts for example there were  credible attempts to create smartphones  as early as 1990. smartphones continued  to advance for the next 15 years but it  wasn't until after the iphone launch  that they transitioned from the  incubation to the growth phase the  growth phase starts when the right mix  of technology talent and community  knowledge comes together it's driven by  reinforcing feedback loop between  infrastructure and applications for  example as the iphone improved better  apps were possible better apps helped  drive iphone sales giving apple more  money to invest enabling even better  apps and so on the tech industry is very  different today than it was in 2008 a  handful of tech incumbents dominate the  internet exerting vast economic and  cultural influence entrenched interest  aggressively respond to new movements  that might someday threaten them he  believes that krypto and web3 will be at  the center of the next cycle that  they've reached a critical mass of  technology talent and community  knowledge in the almost 10 years since  he's been involved in the space the  energy and creativity has never been  higher chris then goes on to talk about  if we are headed for an economic  downturn there are some tactical lessons  from the 2008 era namely preserving  capital and staying focused on your  long-term vision but the main lesson is  to ignore the noise and stay squarely  focused on the product cycle so i think  that chris has got some really  interesting ideas here and many of them  i agree with as you look across market  cycles both up and down what you find is  it the best companies they continue to  innovate they continue to release  products and many times in the bear  markets that's actually where the true  value gets built and eventually starts  to grow into some sort of growth phase  at the end of the day no one knows  what's going to happen in the future we  don't know where many of these  technologies are going to be built which  ones will be adopted on a global basis  and what the actual impact of the  innovation is but what we do know is  that the market cycles are a natural  part of how the world works there are  good times and there are bad times  there's reasons why we get the good  times and there's reasons why we get the  bad times if you want to spend most of  your time trying to analyze why the good  and bad times happen you can do that but  the builders they'll be busy building  actual technology and trying to innovate  trying to figure out what are the  products that we can now create that  previously weren't possible at the end  of the day when people get into bear  markets it usually brings out two  different types of people some people  are trolls and they simply end up  attacking everyone else others are  builders and they put their heads down  they focus and they start creating more  and more value one of the beautiful  parts of bear markets is that they  always end and at the end of the bear  market what we find is who was doing the  work and who was just chirping on the  internet as we continued to watch this  bear market play out looking back in  2008 in the 2000 tech bubble or many  other market cycles that came before it  we can learn a lot of different lessons  but what you find is that many of those  lessons are timeless make sure that you  have the capital to weather the storm  make sure you stay focused on actually  building true innovation and creating  products that people love and last but  not least don't worry about all the  noise  whenever we go into bear markets all the  critics they come out and they're loud  they start celebrating taking victory  laps on the internet  but they'll be quiet again they'll be  quiet once the bear market ends and they  begin to realize this technology is real  and that people around the world are  adopting it so if you're a builder keep  your head down and stay focused if  somebody is chirping leave them alone  bear markets end and technology cycles  continue true innovation doesn't care  what people on the internet are saying  we saw that in 2000 saw it in 2008 and  my guess is we're gonna see it again hey  you did you like this video great we  make five of them a day and post them  here on this channel make sure you  subscribe like the video and see you  next time
 

